---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import Layers from '../../components/Layers.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'House Systems', href: '/explore/house-systems/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="House Systems — why astrologers divide the chart differently"
  description="Whole sign, Placidus, equal house, and others: what changes between house systems, what doesn't, and why there is no single correct answer."
  path="/explore/house-systems/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>House systems</h1>
        <p class="lede">Ask three astrologers which house your Venus is in and you may get two answers. That isn't sloppiness — it's an open question the tradition has never settled.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.src}
        srcset={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.alt}
        width={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.width}
        height={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The problem being solved</h2>
      <p>
        The zodiac is a clean 360° circle divided into twelve equal signs. The
        <a href="/explore/houses/">houses</a> are also twelve divisions — but they're anchored to
        the local horizon at your birth, which is tilted relative to the ecliptic and varies with
        latitude. Converting one geometry into the other has no single obvious solution, so
        astrologers developed several. Each is internally consistent; they simply divide differently.
      </p>

      <h2>The three you'll meet most</h2>
      <h3>Whole sign houses</h3>
      <p>
        The oldest system, standard in Hellenistic astrology and widely revived today. The entire
        sign containing your Ascendant becomes the first house, the next sign the second, and so on
        — houses and signs are identical. Simple, robust at extreme latitudes, and it produces no
        intercepted signs. In this system the Midheaven floats: it may land in the ninth, tenth, or
        eleventh house rather than marking the tenth cusp.
      </p>
      <h3>Placidus</h3>
      <p>
        The most common system in modern Western practice and the default in most software. It
        divides by time rather than space — tracking how long a degree takes to rise. Houses are
        unequal in size, sometimes dramatically so, and the system breaks down at latitudes beyond
        roughly 66°, where some houses become undefined.
      </p>
      <h3>Equal house</h3>
      <p>
        Twelve exactly 30° houses measured from the Ascendant degree. Keeps the Ascendant as the
        first house cusp while giving every house identical size. Like whole sign, it detaches the
        Midheaven from the tenth cusp.
      </p>
      <p>
        Others in regular use include Koch, Porphyry, Regiomontanus (favoured in traditional horary),
        Campanus, and Alcabitius. Each has advocates and a rationale.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.keys} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What actually changes — and what doesn't</h2>
      <p><strong>Unchanged by house system:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Every planet's sign and degree</li>
        <li>Every aspect between planets</li>
        <li>The Ascendant and Midheaven degrees themselves</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Changed by house system:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Which house a planet falls in — sometimes shifting it by one, occasionally more</li>
        <li>Which sign sits on each house cusp, and therefore which planet rules that house</li>
        <li>Whether signs are intercepted (contained entirely within one house) or duplicated</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        So the disagreement is bounded. Most of a chart is identical in every system; what moves is
        the assignment of planets to life areas — which is admittedly not a small thing.
      </p>

      <Layers kind="mo">
        <p>
          I'll tell you which system I'm using and why, and I'm happy to look at a chart in more than
          one when a placement sits near a cusp. That's not indecision — a planet at the edge of two
          houses genuinely does describe a life that straddles two arenas, and reading both is more
          honest than pretending the boundary is crisp.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <Layers kind="differs">
        <p>
          There is no consensus and there may never be. Hellenistic revivalists generally use whole
          sign; most twentieth-century Western astrologers use Placidus; traditional horary
          practitioners often use Regiomontanus. Astrologers who have tested systems against their
          own client work usually settle on one by experience rather than argument — which is a
          reasonable epistemology for a symbolic system, and worth being upfront about.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <h2>What to do about it as a client</h2>
      <p>
        Ask. A good astrologer will name their system without defensiveness. If two readings put
        your Venus in different houses, neither person made an error — they used different methods,
        and the useful question is which reading described your life more accurately.
      </p>
      <p>
        Related: <a href="/explore/angles/">the four angles</a>,
        <a href="/explore/houses/">the twelve houses</a>, and
        <a href="/explore/schools/">schools and traditions</a>.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand heading="Curious which system fits your chart?" body="I'll always tell you which system I'm using and why — and look at more than one when it matters." buttonLabel="See the readings" buttonHref="/readings/" />
</BaseLayout>
